{"causes":["A gradual erosion of traditional fantasy tropes in the 1980s and 1990s, which paved the way for a new generation of authors like George R. R. Martin to experiment with complex world-building and morally ambiguous characters.","The rise of the fantasy genre as a mainstream literary force in the late 20th century, marked by the success of authors like J.R.R. Tolkien and Ursula K. Le Guin.","A growing disillusionment with traditional heroic narratives among readers and writers, which led to a renewed interest in complex, character-driven storytelling."],"effects":["The creation of a vast and dedicated fan base, with enthusiasts worldwide creating their own art, cosplay, and fan fiction inspired by the novel's characters and world.","A significant increase in sales for fantasy novels and authors, as A Game of Thrones helped to popularize the genre and pave the way for future bestsellers like The Wheel of Time and The First Law trilogy.","The development of a new business model for fantasy publishing, with authors like George R. R. Martin successfully leveraging their fans' enthusiasm to create lucrative merchandise and spin-offs."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"george-r-r-martin","sourceName":"George R. R. Martin","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"a-game-of-thrones","targetName":"A Game of Thrones","context":"Published in 1996"},{"sourceSlug":"Neil-Gaiman","sourceName":"Neil Gaiman","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-game-of-thrones","targetName":"A Game of Thrones","context":"American Gods (2001)"},{"sourceSlug":"HBO","sourceName":"HBO","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-game-of-thrones","targetName":"A Game of Thrones","context":"Television adaptation (2011)"},{"sourceSlug":"Amazon","sourceName":"Amazon","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-game-of-thrones","targetName":"A Game of Thrones","context":"Hardcover edition (1999)"},{"sourceSlug":"Barnes & Noble","sourceName":"Barnes & Noble","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-game-of-thrones","targetName":"A Game of Thrones","context":"Paperback edition (2000)"},{"sourceSlug":"Wikipedia","sourceName":"Wikipedia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-game-of-thrones","targetName":"A Game of Thrones","context":"Initial publication information"}],"places":[{"name":"New York City, USA","role":"Publishing hub"},{"name":"London, UK","role":"Literary market influence"},{"name":"Prague, Czech Republic","role":"Location of George R. R. Martin's writing retreats"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: A Game of Thrones","type":"Reference article","slug":"a-game-of-thrones"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1751870","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Game_of_Thrones"],"tags":["literary work","Novels & Prose Fiction","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"A Game of Thrones contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"A Game of Thrones is the first volume of the epic fantasy series A Song of Ice and Fire by George R.R. Martin (born 1948), published in August 1996 by Bantam Books — the founding text of one of the most commercially successful and culturally influential fantasy series in the history of the genre, which has sold over 90 million copies worldwide and was adapted into the HBO television series Game of Thrones (2011–2019), one of the most-watched television series in history. Set in the fictional medieval world of Westeros — a continent in which seasons last for years and supernatural threats lurk beyond an enormous northern wall — A Game of Thrones follows multiple interleaved storylines: the honourable Ned Stark, Lord of Winterfell, drawn into the political intrigue of the royal court; Daenerys Targaryen, exiled princess and heir to the dragon-riding dynasty displaced by a rebellion; and numerous other viewpoint characters caught in the wars, conspiracies, and dynastic struggles over the Iron Throne of Westeros.

A Game of Thrones transformed the epic fantasy genre by introducing a moral and narrative sophistication unprecedented in commercial fantasy — its willingness to kill major viewpoint characters (most famously Ned Stark, the novel's apparent protagonist, executed at the novel's climax), its portrayal of the political consequences of idealism and honour in a world of ruthless cynicism, and its explicit sexual and violent content challenged the conventions of Tolkienian heroic fantasy. Martin's approach — drawing inspiration from the Wars of the Roses and other episodes of medieval dynastic conflict, from Tolkien filtered through the historical novel tradition — produced a fantasy series in which the 'realistic' political and human complexity of historical fiction was combined with the fantastical elements of traditional epic fantasy.

The HBO television adaptation of A Game of Thrones (2011–2019) — one of the most expensive and widely watched television productions in history, peaking at 44 million viewers per episode — took the series from the enthusiastic readership of fantasy literature to global mainstream cultural phenomenon, making Westeros and its characters among the most recognisable fictional settings in contemporary popular culture.","subjects":["Fantasy Literature","Contemporary Era","George R.R. Martin","Epic Fantasy","Television Adaptation","American Fiction","21st Century","Popular Culture"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"A Game of Thrones has had a profound impact on the fantasy genre, influencing numerous authors and works, and cementing its place in popular culture.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
